WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
["Pomp and Circumstance"]

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.000
Life began in the sea.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.000
The water carries oxygen so that creatures can breathe

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.000
and microscopic organisms to provide them with food.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.000
It's a rich world.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.000
It covers three-quarters of the planet,

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.000
and the fish are masters of it.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.000
The world of water is a varied one,

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.000
but the fish are the most important.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.000
They are a varied one, but the fish,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.000
by developing into thousands of different forms,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
exploit almost every part of it.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Collecting different kinds of food requires different shaped bodies,

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:37.000
and they are quite unexpected.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.000
They've developed a multitude of different ways

00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:08.000
to feed themselves through the water.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.000
In size, they vary enormously.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.000
There are giants.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.000
A grouper like this can grow to be twice as long as a man,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.000
and others are so tiny that they can slip inside

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.000
a big fish's mouth and pick its teeth for it.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Fish have developed some surprising ways

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.000
to feed themselves through the water.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Fish have developed some surprising ways

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.000
of finding their way about in this varied underwater world.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.000
The four-eyed fish has eyes divided horizontally

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.000
so that it can look above the surface

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.000
and below it at the same time.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.000
On the other hand, the cave fish,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.000
which normally lives in eternal blackness,

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.000
has no eyes at all.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.000
How did this astounding variety come about?

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.000
What were the earliest fishes like,

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.000
whose descendants now exploit the resources of the seas,

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.000
the lakes and the rivers in such a multitude of different ways?

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:31.000
The answer may lie with one of the simplest organisms in the sea.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.000
It's a tiny, insignificant little blob of jelly.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.000
And amazing, indeed fantastic though it is,

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.000
there are good reasons to suppose that it was a creature like this

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.000
that gave rise to a line which led not only to the fish,

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.000
but through them to the amphibians, reptiles, mammals and man.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:58.000
It's called, not very attractively, but quite accurately, a sea squirt.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:05.000
And to know why, you have to look at it in water.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Its structure is very simple indeed.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Just a U-shaped tube enclosed in jelly.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.000
It sucks water in at the top,

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.000
passes it through a grid inside the body

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.000
that filters out the food particles,

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:24.000
and then squirts it out at the side.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.000
When it first hatches, however, it's rather different.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.000
And here is the clue that links it to fish.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.000
It has a tail with a thin, flexible rod in it.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Little bunches of muscle are attached to the rod

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.000
so that the animal can swim by beating it from side to side.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.000
In front, it has some sensory pits,

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.000
so it has some perception of its surroundings.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.000
We know that this is a very ancient body pattern

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.000
because a fossil creature with both these characters

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:56.000
has been found in rocks 530 million years old.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Here again, those bunches of muscles attached to a rod.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.000
It's larger, but built on the same principles as the young sea squirt.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:09.000
And a creature very like this still survives today.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.000
The lancelet.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.000
This tiny sliver of flesh has no jaws,

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.000
just a mouth surrounded by tentacles.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.000
The bunches of muscles attached to the rod in its back

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.000
enable it to swim with an S-shaped wriggle,

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.000
each bend pushing against the water

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.000
so that the creature moves forward.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.000
Here it's filmed in slow motion.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.000
It's an action that's going to appear again and again

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.000
in what is to come.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.000
Lancelets live half-buried in the bottom of the sea

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:44.000
with just their heads projecting above the gravel

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.000
so that they can filter feed.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:51.000
There's another creature that has the same kind of lifestyle

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.000
and is built on similar lines,

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:56.000
and it swims in the same way as the lancelet.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.000
It's a lamprey, and later in some species

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.000
it will change from filter feeding to a parasitic way of life

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:10.000
using a rasping sucker at its head end.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.000
It extracts oxygen from the water

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.000
and then it's fed to the sea.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.000
It then takes the oxygen from the water

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:22.000
and continues to suck it in at the mouth

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.000
and expel it through gill slits on the neck.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.000
Its close relative, the hagfish, lives in the sea,

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.000
sometimes burying itself in the mud on the bottom,

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:34.000
sometimes fastening itself to fish with its teeth

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:37.000
and eating with a sucker-like mouth.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.000
So, judging from the design of their bodies

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.000
and the way they move them,

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.000
there does seem to be a connection

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.000
between the lancelet and the hagfish and the lamprey.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.000
But although the hagfish looks like a fish, it's not one.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.000
It has no strengthening to that rod in the back,

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:58.000
no real backbone, and no jaws.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.000
Of course, it could be that the reason

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:08.000
that the lamprey and the hagfish haven't got any jaws

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.000
is not that they are primitive creatures

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:13.000
that never developed them,

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:16.000
but that they are degenerate ones that lost them.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.000
The way to find the answer to that is to look in the rocks.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:23.000
The earliest fossils of shells and corals

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:26.000
appear about 600 million years ago.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:29.000
And then for 200 million years,

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:32.000
there's no sign whatever of any backbone animals.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.000
But then, suddenly, they appear.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:38.000
And some of the finest specimens have been found

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.000
in these very ancient rocks

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:43.000
in the west of England.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:46.000
And these creatures have no jaws either.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:49.000
They have scales down their flanks

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:52.000
and a head covered by a heavy bony shield.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:55.000
And they must have swum by wriggling this body

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:58.000
and pushing their head along the bottom.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.000
And at the front, between two small eyes, there is a nostril.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:05.000
In fact, it's a kind of lamprey in armour.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:08.000
At the time of which we're talking,

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.000
about 600 million years ago,

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:14.000
the face of the Earth was not at all like what it is today.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:17.000
The relationships of the continents, the ocean basins,

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:20.000
the coastlines, all were very different.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:23.000
Only in a few places can you today get a clear picture

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:26.000
of what those ancient shores were like.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:29.000
And here, in Western Australia, in the Kimberley Ranges,

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:31.000
there's one of them.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:38.000
RUMBLING

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:44.000
Rising above the parched and sandy scrub,

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:47.000
there are strangely shaped outcrops of rock.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:55.000
Those bluffs owe their curious shape

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.000
not to the erosion of wind and rain,

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:02.000
but to the labours millions of years ago of coral polyps.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:12.000
We are flying over an ancient seabed,

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.000
with the original coast and the land behind it,

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:18.000
now a rocky plateau, stretching away in the distance.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:21.000
Once, this plain was covered by a shallow blue lagoon

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:25.000
in which corals built their great constructions of limestone.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:28.000
Over the millennia, rivers eroded the continent nearby,

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.000
washed down the sand and mud,

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:34.000
and deposited it over the seafloor.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:38.000
So the lagoon slowly silted up and the sea retreated.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:42.000
Then the continent rose, rain and sun eroded the mudstones,

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:46.000
and eventually the coral reefs were exposed once more

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:56.000
to the cliffs on a sun-baked plain.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:00.000
And here I'm walking on the ancient seabed.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.000
The surface of the sea would have been close to the top

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:07.000
of those reefs over there, so here I would have been

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:09.000
about 200 feet down.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:13.000
And the sediments that lay on the bottom of that ancient sea

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:19.000
are still here, turned into sandstones and mudstones.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:22.000
And in them are the remains of the creatures

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:25.000
that lived in those seas.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:28.000
Here is one that I picked up only a few minutes ago.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:32.000
It's the scale of a huge fish.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:38.000
And this is the flank of a smaller fish with many scales on it.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.000
And this, which is perhaps the least impressive of all,

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:46.000
is actually the most interesting, because this is a fossil skull.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:49.000
There is the line of its lower jaw.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:53.000
And if this nodule is treated with acids,

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:56.000
the matrix will be eroded away and expose

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:03.000
the perfectly preserved bones of the skull.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:06.000
These creatures, 400 million years old,

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:09.000
were a considerable advance on the lancelets and the lampreys,

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:12.000
for these had true jaws.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:16.000
And on their edges, the scales grew particularly long and sharp

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:21.000
so that the fish could bite and cut.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:25.000
Jaws armed with teeth enabled the fish to become

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:28.000
very effective food gatherers, and so grow into large

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:30.000
and powerful creatures.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.000
And some of them became monsters.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:37.000
Judging from the size of these gigantic teeth,

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:41.000
the shark was about 45 feet long.

00:11:41.000 --> 00:12:10.000
It's extinct, but its relatives are very much alive.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:13.000
Sensitive pits in the front of the head, nostrils,

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:16.000
enable them to detect their prey from great distances.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:20.000
The hammerhead shark is said to be particularly sensitive,

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:23.000
and this may explain the grotesque shape of its head.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:27.000
There's a nostril at the end of each side of the hammer,

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:31.000
and the fish habitually swings its head from side to side,

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:34.000
so that when the scent is equally strong in both nostrils,

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:39.000
then it must know that its prey lies straight ahead.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:43.000
That rod in the back has now been strengthened with cartilage,

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:46.000
and the entire skeleton of sharks is built

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:49.000
from this soft, light material.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:53.000
They still swim like the lancelets with sideways beats of their body,

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:57.000
which are restricted mostly to the back half and to the tail.

00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:01.000
The thrust created tends to drive the nose downwards,

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:05.000
and to compensate for that, sharks have a pair of horizontal fins

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:08.000
on either side of the front, like the veins of a submarine.

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:11.000
But these fins are stiff and inflexible.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:14.000
The shark can't twist them vertically to act as brakes.

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:19.000
Indeed, a charging shark can't stop, only swerve to one side,

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:21.000
nor can it swim backwards.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:24.000
Furthermore, since its body is heavier than water,

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:28.000
if it stopped swimming, a shark would sink.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:36.000
The wobbegong, a shark from Australian waters,

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:39.000
has a tendency to do just that.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:42.000
It's largely abandoned the effort of perpetually swimming

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:45.000
to keep in mid-water, and has settled on the seafloor

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:48.000
where it leads a more restful life.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:01.000
The transition from continuous swimming in the open sea

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.000
to a life more or less permanently on the bottom

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:07.000
is seen in a series of fishes.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:12.000
The dogfish is very shark-like.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:16.000
The angelshark, however, is rather more flattened

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:20.000
with wide side fins and a rather smaller tail.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:29.000
The ray has flattened its body to an extreme degree,

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:33.000
dispensing with that rear engine the powerful thrashing tail

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:36.000
and extending the lateral fins so that their ripples

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:39.000
can take over the job of propelling the fish through the water.

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:43.000
And it spends most of its time lying on the bottom.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:49.000
MUSIC PLAYS

00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:01.000
A light dusting of gravel does wonders for camouflage.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:10.000
The sawfish shark is another bottom-liver,

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:14.000
and it uses its extraordinary blade like a double-edged scythe,

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:18.000
excavating in the sand and gravel for shells and crabs,

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:21.000
and sometimes flailing through a shoal of fish,

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:25.000
slashing them so that they fall injured and can be eaten.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:29.000
So, bodies with cartilaginous skeletons

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:32.000
developed into two main shapes.

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:40.000
One shaped like sharks and wide ones like rays and skates.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:56.000
But having learned, as it were, to live on the bottom,

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:59.000
some rays took off again.

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:03.000
The two side fins are very effective motors for mid-water swimming,

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:06.000
provided that speed is not needed.

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:09.000
So they're very suitable for fish like the manta ray

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:11.000
that drifts through the surface waters,

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:13.000
filter-feeding on plankton.

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:16.000
The blades on either side of the manta's head

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:20.000
help to channel the food-bearing water into the slot-like mouth.

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:23.000
The manta cannot swim much faster than this,

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:26.000
but it wouldn't help its feeding very much, even if it did,

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:29.000
because the water can't flow through the sieve in the gill slits

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:32.000
any faster than it's doing now.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:44.000
Filter-feeding in the surface of the ocean

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:47.000
is clearly a very effective way of life.

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:51.000
It doesn't require much energy, there's an unlimited supply of food,

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:55.000
and some of the fish that have taken to it have become very large indeed.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:57.880
The

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:57.880
fishing shark grows to a length of 15 metres,

00:16:57.880 --> 00:16:59.880
45 feet.

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:03.880
Only one fish today is any bigger, the whale shark,

00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:06.880
and that, too, is a filter-feeder.

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:14.880
And there, clinging under its tail,

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:18.880
is a primitive, jawless lamprey, sucking at its flesh,

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:21.880
a reminder of the fish's remote past,

00:17:21.880 --> 00:17:24.880
a relative of the earliest swimmers.

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:31.880
Another filter-feeder, the paddlefish,

00:17:31.880 --> 00:17:35.880
but this is only very distantly related to the sharks and rays.

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:39.880
400 million years ago, right at the beginning of fish history,

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:43.880
a group started constructing their skeletons not of cartilage,

00:17:43.880 --> 00:17:48.880
but of solid bone, and the ancestors of the paddlefish were among them.

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:52.880
And another of these primitive bony fish, the sturgeon.

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:56.880
Not only does it have bone in its internal skeleton,

00:17:56.880 --> 00:18:00.880
it also has heavy, bony scales in its skin.

00:18:00.880 --> 00:18:04.880
It's the eggs of this fish that are made into caviar.

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:18.880
It still swims very like a shark,

00:18:18.880 --> 00:18:21.880
and the eggs are made of a very strong,

00:18:21.880 --> 00:18:25.880
very strong, very strong, very strong, very strong,

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:29.880
very strong, very strong, very strong, very strong,

00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:32.880
but it still swims very like a shark

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:35.880
with sweeps of its hind body and tail,

00:18:35.880 --> 00:18:38.880
and the tail looks shark-like too.

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:44.880
Soon after the bony fish first appeared,

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:47.880
they spread from the seas up the rivers

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:50.880
to colonise the fresh waters of the world.

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:59.880
It was a revolution that was to have revolutionary consequences.

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:03.880
The waters of rivers and lakes are shallow compared to the sea,

00:19:03.880 --> 00:19:06.880
and often, as a consequence, they get quite warm.

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:08.880
And the warmer water becomes,

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:11.880
the less oxygen it can hold dissolved in it.

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:15.880
That presents a serious problem to any fish living there.

00:19:15.880 --> 00:19:18.880
How are they to breathe?

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:21.880
Well, there's one of them, the polypterus,

00:19:21.880 --> 00:19:24.880
and this is its solution.

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:27.880
It gulps air and then absorbs the gaseous oxygen

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:30.880
from a pouch that leads off its gut.

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:34.880
In other words, it has developed a very simple lung.

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:37.880
But an air-filled pouch within the body

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:40.880
brings another incidental advantage.

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:42.880
It gives buoyancy.

00:19:42.880 --> 00:19:45.880
So the bony fish acquire a swim bladder,

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:48.880
which is located inside the body.

00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:54.880
Now the elements of the modern fish have been assembled.

00:19:54.880 --> 00:19:56.880
A swim bladder for buoyancy,

00:19:56.880 --> 00:19:59.880
a backbone with muscles attached for strength,

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:01.880
and gills for breathing.

00:20:01.880 --> 00:20:04.880
And for further precision and control,

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:06.880
there is the lateral line,

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:09.880
a row of tiny pits that are sensitive to pressures

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:11.880
and currents in the water.

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:14.880
And so the modern bony fish, like this trout,

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:16.880
are able to breathe.

00:20:24.880 --> 00:20:27.880
This perfection of senses and control of movement

00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:44.880
is critical when a pike is on the hunt for roach.

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:47.880
With buoyancy provided by the swim bladder,

00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:50.880
the fins can be used entirely for fine adjustments

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:57.880
as it hovers.

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:47.880
At normal speed,

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:50.880
it's almost impossible to see what happens.

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:52.880
It's so fast.

00:21:59.880 --> 00:22:01.880
Slowed down, it's possible to see

00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:04.880
the enormous acceleration and accuracy.

00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:07.880
The actual bite only lasts a split second,

00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:28.880
and the prey goes straight in.

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:33.880
I'm standing on the brink of one of the most densely populated parts

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:37.880
in the sea. I'm on the edge of a coral reef at a low tide.

00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:41.880
A few feet out there, the bottom sinks dramatically,

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:44.880
and there you'll find an abundance of life of all kinds,

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:48.880
microscopic plants, invertebrates, corals,

00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:51.880
and, of course, a multitude of fish

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:55.880
that come there to harvest this rich source of food.

00:22:55.880 --> 00:22:58.880
Each kind of fish has its own particular place in this mosaic,

00:22:58.880 --> 00:23:00.880
its own particular food,

00:23:00.880 --> 00:23:04.880
and each has, in consequence, developed its own way of swimming,

00:23:04.880 --> 00:23:19.880
its own way of using its fins.

00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:22.880
The huge number of fish that swarm on the reef,

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:25.880
harvesting the great variety of food that it offers,

00:23:25.880 --> 00:23:28.880
causes considerable social problems.

00:23:28.880 --> 00:23:32.880
Each species has its own particular niche on the reef

00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:34.880
and is designed accordingly.

00:23:34.880 --> 00:23:38.880
Many are slim for slipping through the tangle of coral.

00:23:38.880 --> 00:23:42.880
Others, like the cowfish, have a rigid box of bony plates

00:23:42.880 --> 00:23:47.880
and can stop dead with precise control from its fins.

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:53.880
The trigger sticks its fin-free front half between coral branches to feed.

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:57.880
The angelfish picks off small morsels from the surface of corals,

00:23:57.880 --> 00:24:04.880
once again with perfect control.

00:24:04.880 --> 00:24:07.880
And this butterflyfish has elongated jaws

00:24:07.880 --> 00:24:10.880
that enable it to probe into narrow crevices

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:14.880
with the accuracy of forceps.

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:18.880
For turning sharply, banking steeply, or simply flapping along,

00:24:18.880 --> 00:24:22.880
most coral fishes have been able to abandon the S-shaped wriggle.

00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:25.880
They've deployed their fins and adjusted their bodies

00:24:25.880 --> 00:24:28.880
to live in this particular world.

00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:40.880
No shark could do this,

00:24:40.880 --> 00:24:45.880
but then they are adapted to a different kind of life.

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:53.880
The pufferfish doesn't wriggle its body,

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:56.880
but it does flex its fins and to great effect.

00:24:56.880 --> 00:25:01.880
The S-shape action is now being used there.

00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:09.880
And fins have another important role, as flags.

00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:11.880
In such a mixed and dense crowd,

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:14.880
it's very much to the advantage of every individual fish

00:25:14.880 --> 00:25:18.880
to proclaim its presence and identity from among the throng,

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:20.880
so that rivals will be aware

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:23.880
that this particular food patch has got an owner.

00:25:23.880 --> 00:25:26.880
And the same markings will also serve, when the time comes,

00:25:26.880 --> 00:25:30.880
to attract a mate of the right species.

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:37.880
The sharks and rays have eyes that, though they see shapes very well,

00:25:37.880 --> 00:25:39.880
are largely blind to colour.

00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:41.880
It's hardly surprising, therefore,

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:44.880
that they are by and large drab-coloured creatures.

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:47.880
But the bonyfish have excellent colour vision,

00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:51.880
so they're able to signal to one another with stripes and spots

00:25:51.880 --> 00:25:55.880
and blotches and in the most wonderful variety of colours.

00:25:55.880 --> 00:25:58.880
The coralfish can risk making themselves conspicuous

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:01.880
because the reef is full of crevices and corners

00:26:01.880 --> 00:26:05.880
where they can dart to safety if danger threatens.

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:15.880
Away from the reef, however, the sea is a dangerous place,

00:26:15.880 --> 00:26:19.880
for there there is nowhere to hide, except among your fellows.

00:26:25.880 --> 00:26:28.880
And these are designed for a very different way of life.

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:31.880
Fast swimming, fast feeding in the open sea

00:26:31.880 --> 00:26:34.880
with plankton at the base of the food chain.

00:26:34.880 --> 00:26:45.880
And it's that wriggling body action that pushes them along.

00:26:51.880 --> 00:26:54.880
Open water fish often form huge shoals,

00:26:54.880 --> 00:26:57.880
and this may be for safety's sake.

00:26:57.880 --> 00:26:59.880
The drifting, darting multitudes of fish

00:26:59.880 --> 00:27:02.880
may tend to baffle and confuse predators.

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:05.880
And if you meet a shark on your own, it'll go for you.

00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:09.880
But if you are with others, your chances are much better.

00:27:20.880 --> 00:27:22.880
From the plankton to the small fish

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:26.880
and on up the food chain to the big fish.

00:27:26.880 --> 00:27:29.880
In the open ocean, speed is of great value.

00:27:29.880 --> 00:27:33.880
And since water is very dense, 800 times more so than air,

00:27:33.880 --> 00:27:37.880
streamlining is of the greatest importance to fish.

00:27:37.880 --> 00:27:39.880
Both hunters and the fish they pursue

00:27:39.880 --> 00:27:41.880
have developed very similar shapes,

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:46.880
pointed in front and tapering to a two-bladed symmetrical tail at the back.

00:27:46.880 --> 00:27:51.880
Barracuda, among the most voracious and swift of the bony fish.

00:27:51.880 --> 00:27:55.880
And this is a hunter's eye view of a fish that escapes

00:27:55.880 --> 00:28:00.880
not by swimming fast, but in a quite different way.

00:28:02.880 --> 00:28:04.880
It's a flying fish.

00:28:04.880 --> 00:28:07.880
Its front pair of fins are greatly enlarged

00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:09.880
so that with a flick of its tail,

00:28:09.880 --> 00:28:21.880
it launches itself into the air and out of the hunter's sight.

00:28:21.880 --> 00:28:23.880
This is the flight in slow motion.

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:26.880
The fish is already swimming fast when it comes to the surface

00:28:26.880 --> 00:28:29.880
and it takes off, helped by the beating tail,

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:31.880
which has a specially enlarged lower lobe.

00:28:31.880 --> 00:28:39.880
The front fins are then spread to assist the glide.

00:28:39.880 --> 00:28:42.880
Occasionally, they dip their tails into the surface

00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:45.880
to give themselves an additional boost,

00:28:45.880 --> 00:28:50.880
and so they can sometimes fly for several hundred metres.

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:56.880
Some fish have sought safety by going not upwards, but downwards.

00:28:56.880 --> 00:29:00.880
These eggs that float in astronomic numbers on the surface of the sea

00:29:00.880 --> 00:29:04.880
during the summer have come from one of the bottom dwellers.

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:07.880
Only one in a hundred thousand will survive,

00:29:07.880 --> 00:29:11.880
but those that do will pass through a most extraordinary transformation

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:13.880
before they become adult.

00:29:18.880 --> 00:29:21.880
After about a week, they hatch into what looks like

00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:23.880
a fairly normal kind of fish fry.

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:26.880
They hang near the surface where it's warm

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:29.880
and there's a lot of oxygen, feeding on microorganisms.

00:29:29.880 --> 00:29:32.880
Each is not much bigger than a pinhead.

00:29:45.880 --> 00:29:48.880
Each one still contains a tiny bag of yolk

00:29:48.880 --> 00:29:51.880
that will sustain it for a day or two more.

00:29:53.880 --> 00:29:56.880
The young fish has eyes on either side of its head,

00:29:56.880 --> 00:29:59.880
but they won't remain that way for long.

00:29:59.880 --> 00:30:03.880
Its body deepens as it begins to feed, and its stomach swells,

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:06.880
and it develops a swim bladder.

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:14.880
Its eyes are beginning to look a little lopsided.

00:30:14.880 --> 00:30:17.880
One is higher than the other.

00:30:20.880 --> 00:30:23.880
Now they've developed pigment, but only on one flank,

00:30:23.880 --> 00:30:27.880
and they swim on their sides with that coloured flank upwards.

00:30:27.880 --> 00:30:30.880
These are going to be flatfish, turbot.

00:30:30.880 --> 00:30:34.880
Place, soul and flounder also go through such a transformation.

00:30:38.880 --> 00:30:41.880
And they finally settle on the bottom.

00:30:46.880 --> 00:30:49.880
One eye is now on the edge of the fish.

00:30:49.880 --> 00:30:52.880
Now the transformation is complete,

00:30:52.880 --> 00:30:55.880
and the fish has lost that swim bladder,

00:30:55.880 --> 00:30:59.880
for buoyancy is a positive hindrance on the sea bottom.

00:30:59.880 --> 00:31:03.880
A bony fish has joined the skates and the rays on the sea floor

00:31:03.880 --> 00:31:07.880
by the simple, if drastic, expedient of lying on its side

00:31:07.880 --> 00:31:10.880
and moving one eye right round its body.

00:31:13.880 --> 00:31:16.880
Many other bony fish have abandoned the swim bladder

00:31:16.880 --> 00:31:20.880
and settled down. Each has found its own way of adapting to life

00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:23.880
where skill in swimming is of less importance

00:31:23.880 --> 00:31:27.880
than an ability to merge into the background of the sea floor.

00:31:27.880 --> 00:31:31.880
And so fins can be used for all kinds of other purposes.

00:31:31.880 --> 00:31:34.880
This looks like a rock lying on the bottom,

00:31:34.880 --> 00:31:39.880
but it has a gill, an eye and an upturned mouth.

00:31:39.880 --> 00:31:43.880
It's a stonefish, a hunter that relies on invisibility

00:31:43.880 --> 00:31:46.880
to catch its prey unawares,

00:31:46.880 --> 00:31:50.880
and its fins are coloured and shaped to help its camouflage.

00:31:52.880 --> 00:31:57.880
The anglerfish uses its fins not for swimming but for walking,

00:31:57.880 --> 00:32:02.880
and the front spine of its dorsal fin is a fishing rod.

00:32:04.880 --> 00:32:07.880
With that lure, it attracts unsuspecting creatures

00:32:07.880 --> 00:32:13.880
within range of its mouth.

00:32:17.880 --> 00:32:20.880
The bearded ghoul uses its fins for defence.

00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:22.880
Without a membrane between them,

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:26.880
they are no longer of any use as stabilisers when swimming,

00:32:26.880 --> 00:32:29.880
and instead they are sharp and tipped with poison.

00:32:29.880 --> 00:32:31.880
Very effective protection.

00:32:31.880 --> 00:32:37.880
It's just what you need if you're lying on the bottom.

00:32:41.880 --> 00:32:45.880
The gurnard uses some of the rays of its front pair of fins

00:32:45.880 --> 00:33:01.880
as delicate legs for finding food in the gravel.

00:33:06.880 --> 00:33:10.880
Fish like those live in comparatively shallow waters,

00:33:10.880 --> 00:33:14.880
100 feet, 30 metres, something like that.

00:33:14.880 --> 00:33:17.880
The fair world is a heavily inhabited one,

00:33:17.880 --> 00:33:19.880
and also quite a bright one,

00:33:19.880 --> 00:33:23.880
because the water is shallow enough to receive light from the sun.

00:33:23.880 --> 00:33:26.880
It's also one that's comparatively familiar to us.

00:33:26.880 --> 00:33:29.880
For one thing, all the sea fish that we come from it,

00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:33.880
for another, hundreds of thousands of people regularly visit it

00:33:33.880 --> 00:33:35.880
wearing aqualungs.

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:41.880
But in fact, it is only a tiny proportion of the seas of the world.

00:33:41.880 --> 00:33:45.880
The depths of the oceans are very, very much deeper than that.

00:33:45.880 --> 00:33:49.880
And to visit those deep waters, you can't go down an aqualung.

00:33:49.880 --> 00:33:53.880
You have to use something like this, a submersible.

00:33:53.880 --> 00:33:57.880
These crafts work on the sea floor, helping in the drilling for oil,

00:33:57.880 --> 00:34:01.880
and they give a splendid view of what's happening at depth,

00:34:01.880 --> 00:34:05.880
both to oil engineers and to fish watchers.

00:34:12.880 --> 00:34:17.880
The gurnard is a very, very, very, very, very, very, very, very,

00:34:17.880 --> 00:34:21.880
very, very, very, very, very, very, very, very, very, very,

00:34:21.880 --> 00:34:23.880
very, very, very, very сound.

00:34:34.880 --> 00:34:38.880
There's a highly sensitive television camera mounted on the outside

00:34:38.880 --> 00:34:41.880
of the hull, with a monitor screen in the cockpit,

00:34:41.880 --> 00:34:43.880
and spotlights to illuminate places

00:34:43.880 --> 00:34:47.880
that the sun's rays have never reached.

00:34:47.880 --> 00:34:54.760
As we go down it gets darker and darker and the pressure increases too very

00:34:54.760 --> 00:35:01.160
quickly. By the time we are 500 feet the loading on this viewing dome here will

00:35:01.160 --> 00:35:09.000
be about 70 tons and it also gets colder and colder and at one point and the

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:13.920
precise depth varies according to where we are in the world between say 20

00:35:13.920 --> 00:35:20.880
meters which is about 60 feet and 150 meters 450 feet it suddenly gets very

00:35:20.880 --> 00:35:26.880
much colder indeed and dropped to about 5 degrees above freezing. That point is

00:35:26.880 --> 00:35:33.360
called the thermocline and it's a kind of frontier in the ocean separating two

00:35:33.360 --> 00:35:38.440
very different worlds between which there is very little traffic. Above there

00:35:38.440 --> 00:35:43.960
is the sunlit warm waters near the surface which have their own circulation

00:35:43.960 --> 00:35:49.960
and below the thermocline there is the black near freezing world of the ocean

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:56.040
depths and there they live very different fish indeed.

00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:04.920
This is part of the world that man is only just beginning to explore. Until a

00:36:04.920 --> 00:36:09.320
few years ago most of our knowledge of these creatures came from specimens that

00:36:09.320 --> 00:36:13.600
had been hauled up in dredges but as they came up the great changes in

00:36:13.600 --> 00:36:17.360
pressure and temperature usually distorted their bodies and they quickly

00:36:17.360 --> 00:36:22.920
died. Only now from such craft as the submersibles are we beginning to get an

00:36:22.920 --> 00:36:29.000
accurate idea of what life is really like in the deeper parts of the oceans.

00:36:29.000 --> 00:36:34.640
A shark built on the same pattern as its relatives above. Like most of the

00:36:34.640 --> 00:36:40.080
inhabitants of these oxygen poor waters it moves comparatively slowly probably

00:36:40.080 --> 00:36:45.640
never meeting a boundary or a barrier in the endless deep sea.

00:36:47.360 --> 00:36:53.280
A red prawn doubtless food for some big fish.

00:36:59.200 --> 00:37:03.880
An extraordinary relative of the prawn another crustacean called an ostracod

00:37:03.880 --> 00:37:09.800
fossils of species very like these have been found in extremely ancient rocks so

00:37:09.800 --> 00:37:15.960
we know that they were here long before the fish arrived. This fish still uses

00:37:15.960 --> 00:37:22.280
that antique way of swimming with s-shaped undulations of its body.

00:37:22.280 --> 00:37:37.960
A fang tooth one of the hunters of this lightless world. Our knowledge of the

00:37:37.960 --> 00:37:41.880
fish of these depths is still very fragmentary many species have never been

00:37:41.880 --> 00:37:48.240
filmed and we know them only from a few mangled specimens and still photographs.

00:37:48.240 --> 00:37:54.200
This bait on a line is suspended from a rod dangling in front of an upper jaw

00:37:54.200 --> 00:38:01.360
lined with needle teeth. It's another kind of angler fish. There are so few

00:38:01.360 --> 00:38:06.000
animals at these depths that when a meal arrives a hunter must make quite sure of

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:08.520
catching it.

00:38:08.520 --> 00:38:18.360
This angler normally looks like this but when it's had its meal its stomach

00:38:18.360 --> 00:38:25.400
becomes hugely distended. The gulper is little more than a swimming mouth also

00:38:25.400 --> 00:38:29.640
with a stomach capable of great extension. The bigger the stomach can go

00:38:29.640 --> 00:38:36.760
the wider the choice of prey and meals may be few and far between. Many fish

00:38:36.760 --> 00:38:42.600
produce lights in this blackness some as face patches or dots along the sides. At

00:38:42.600 --> 00:38:49.240
night many of them move up to shallower water where of course it's still dark.

00:38:57.160 --> 00:39:02.600
With special light-sensitive cameras and a little illumination you can just make

00:39:02.600 --> 00:39:08.440
out the fish that are producing these lights they're called flashlight fish.

00:39:24.080 --> 00:39:29.200
With no illumination at all they become disembodied green spots again

00:39:29.200 --> 00:39:36.160
mysteriously circulating in the blackness. The light is produced by

00:39:36.160 --> 00:39:41.240
bacteria which live in this one patch of skin and glow as a normal byproduct of

00:39:41.240 --> 00:39:48.360
the chemistry of their life processes. The light may serve the flashlight fish

00:39:48.360 --> 00:39:53.120
in several ways. It may be assigned to other members of the show, it may baffle

00:39:53.120 --> 00:39:57.640
predators, after fish switches off its light it immediately darts away to a

00:39:57.640 --> 00:40:01.960
different position or it may simply be a method of finding your way around in the

00:40:01.960 --> 00:40:04.280
blackness.

00:40:12.520 --> 00:40:18.400
The problem of finding the way in the dark faces other fish too. They live in

00:40:18.400 --> 00:40:23.720
turbid waters and under a thick carpet of floating vegetation not an easy place

00:40:23.720 --> 00:40:41.240
to find them. This somewhat surprising piece of apparatus is the latest device

00:40:41.240 --> 00:40:46.920
developed and designed by research workers who are interested in electric

00:40:46.920 --> 00:40:53.000
fishes. This plastic tube has got two leads which come up through this cable

00:40:53.000 --> 00:40:59.840
along here to this extraordinary hat. There they go into this amplifier and on

00:40:59.840 --> 00:41:06.200
the brim of the hat there's a loudspeaker here and very thoughtfully a

00:41:06.200 --> 00:41:12.160
counterweight here so that when I put the hat on my head it doesn't flop over

00:41:12.160 --> 00:41:19.240
one ear and then if I turn on the amplifier the speaker is next to my ear. I

00:41:19.240 --> 00:41:26.360
have one hand free and the wires will pick up the signals of those electric

00:41:26.360 --> 00:41:31.160
fishes and I can hear them as a series of clicks.

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:47.800
The fish produce their electricity from stacks of plate-shaped cells embedded

00:41:47.800 --> 00:41:53.640
in jelly that lie in a column along each flank like batteries. Each fish sends out

00:41:53.640 --> 00:41:57.720
a particular kind of discharge and each makes a different sound on the loud

00:41:57.720 --> 00:42:03.360
speakers so these may be another kind of call sign like the Morse code of the

00:42:03.360 --> 00:42:09.240
flashlight fish a way of proclaiming identity. They also certainly help the

00:42:09.240 --> 00:42:14.960
fish in navigation. Each creates in the water around it a weak electric field.

00:42:14.960 --> 00:42:20.200
Any other solid object in the water causes a change in that field and the

00:42:20.200 --> 00:42:25.200
fish have special pores space out over their bodies which detect such

00:42:25.200 --> 00:42:30.200
alterations. As a result they're able to find their way about in total darkness

00:42:30.200 --> 00:42:45.840
and they can swim just as accurately backwards as they can forwards.

00:42:45.840 --> 00:42:51.120
Electricity has evolved independently in many fishes from South America and

00:42:51.120 --> 00:42:57.240
Africa in rivers lakes and also in the sea. In some the tail muscles are used

00:42:57.240 --> 00:43:02.360
others the head area and even the eye muscles.

00:43:08.720 --> 00:43:13.360
A straight knife-shaped body is characteristic of all these fish and it

00:43:13.360 --> 00:43:17.720
may be that it's important to keep the body stiff in this position in order to

00:43:17.720 --> 00:43:22.680
maintain an accurate output of navigational signals and the fins do the

00:43:22.680 --> 00:43:29.880
maneuvering. Some frequently rest wedged between plant stems.

00:43:52.680 --> 00:44:07.760
Most of the discharges produced by those electric fish are extremely weak. You

00:44:07.760 --> 00:44:12.040
couldn't possibly detect them without special amplifying equipment but that is

00:44:12.040 --> 00:44:18.040
very much not the case with all of them. This is the most powerful electric fish

00:44:18.040 --> 00:44:24.480
of all the famous electric eel from South America. This has two kinds of

00:44:24.480 --> 00:44:28.760
electricity not only does it have batteries which produce the discharges

00:44:28.760 --> 00:44:33.560
used for navigation but it's also capable of delivering a massive electric

00:44:33.560 --> 00:44:38.560
shock which it stuns its prey with and which is quite sufficient to throw me

00:44:38.560 --> 00:44:45.200
on my back if I were not wearing rubber gloves. And I can demonstrate that

00:44:45.200 --> 00:44:51.080
electric shock by tapping him near his head near his tail with these electrodes

00:44:51.080 --> 00:44:56.080
which will then if he gives a shock light up these bulbs and the more powerful

00:44:56.080 --> 00:45:04.840
the shock the more bulbs he will light. There's a rapid output of volts peaking

00:45:04.840 --> 00:45:10.680
in this case at about 400. Four bulbs were lit. Of all backbone animals fish

00:45:10.680 --> 00:45:15.880
are the only ones to produce electricity in their bodies.

00:45:17.640 --> 00:45:23.520
So the bony fish one way or another have managed to colonize all the waters of

00:45:23.520 --> 00:45:28.200
the world from the black depths of the sea to inland rivers and lakes even

